The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!local_node)
    return 0;
if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV) {
    pr_err("failed to send bye cmd\n");
    return ret;
}

Reason:
When a BYE packet is received, the function `ctrl_cmd_bye()` should free the associated `qrtr_node` after processing. However, the two early `return` statements exit the function without calling `kfree(node)` or removing the node from the Xarray. This leaks the node memory. Additionally, the final `return 0` also never frees the node.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!local_node) {
    ret = 0;
    goto delete_node;
}
if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV) {
    pr_err("failed to send bye cmd\n");
    goto delete_node;
}
delete_node:
    xa_erase(&nodes, from->sq_node);
    kfree(node);
    return ret;

Reason:
The fix introduces a `goto delete_node` label that ensures the node is removed from the Xarray (`xa_erase`) and its memory freed (`kfree(node)`) in all exit paths, including when `local_node` is NULL or when `kernel_sendmsg` fails. The return value `ret` is preserved (set to 0 for the NULL case and after success, or the original error for the send failure case) and properly returned after cleanup. This eliminates the memory leak.

